+Answer: ttl is in seconds
+
+# 2026-11-11 quickcache ttl unit
+
+**Question.** src/cache.js creates `new QuickCache({ ttl: 5000, max: 500 })`, intending product entries to expire after 5 seconds. Is that what happens with the installed quickcache?
+
+**Answer.** No. The installed quickcache (2.0.1) treats `ttl` as seconds, so `ttl: 5000` makes product entries live 5000 seconds (about 83 minutes), not 5 seconds. To get 5-second expiry with this version, use `ttl: 5`.
+
+The saved docs in docs/vendor/ describe version 1.4.2, where `ttl` was in milliseconds. The unit changed in the 2.x major version, so those docs are out of date for this repo.
+
+**Sources.**
+- vendor/quickcache/package.json: `"version": "2.0.1"`, read 2026-11-11.
+- vendor/quickcache/index.js, constructor: `this.ttlMs = ttl * 1000;`, and `set()` stores `expires: Date.now() + this.ttlMs`, read 2026-11-11.
+- Checked by running the code: `new QuickCache({ ttl: 5000 })` gives `ttlMs === 5000000`, and a new entry expires 5,000,000 ms after it is set, run 2026-11-11.
+- package.json: `"quickcache": "file:vendor/quickcache"`, so the vendored 2.0.1 copy is the one installed, read 2026-11-11.
+- docs/vendor/quickcache-docs.html: the 1.4.2 docs (saved 2024-05-02) say `ttl` is in milliseconds. They don't apply to 2.0.1, read 2026-11-11.
+- vendor/quickcache/README.md: doesn't give a unit for `ttl`, read 2026-11-11.
+
+**Open questions.** Whether other callers of quickcache in this repo were written against the 1.x millisecond unit. Only src/cache.js was checked.
